preConditionHook:
// SCRATCH AT BREAKEVEN AFTER A BAD EXCURSION. Once this position's open loss has
// at some point been worse than adverseDollars, leave as soon as the close comes
// back to the entry price. The adverse-excursion gate is what makes the rule
// real: V21-02's half on its own ("once the market gets back to their very first
// entry, they exit") fires on the first profitable bar and is degenerate, so
// T15-50's gate governs.
//
// ENGINE PRIMITIVE NOTE: there is no ctx.MinPositionProfit() -- the mirror of
// MaxPositionProfit -- so the running minimum is kept here. It is reset on
// BarsSinceEntry() == 0 and NOT on MarketPosition() == 0: BarsSinceEntry is
// VERIFIED as 0 on the fill bar and as RESTARTING THROUGH A REVERSAL, which is
// exactly the per-position boundary MaxPositionProfit carries and which a flat
// test misses, since a reversal never passes through flat (skill section 4).
// While flat BarsSinceEntry is also 0, so minProf is re-seeded on every flat
// bar; that is harmless and is what leaves it correct on the fill bar -- the
// same convention slices 09 and 10 use to capture signal-bar values.
//
// ONE BEHAVIOURAL DIFFERENCE FROM A TRUE MinPositionProfit REMAINS and cannot be
// removed here: OpenPositionProfit is marked at the last close (VERIFIED, and net of the
// entry side's costs), so minProf is the worst bar-close excursion, not the
// intrabar low water mark the accessor would report.
//
// Not BreakevenStop, which arms on peak PROFIT and exits at EntryPrice - buffer:
// that arms after the trade has gone WELL, this one after it has gone BADLY.
// Sides are MIRRORED, not the slice's "same" -- the EntryPrice comparison is
// directional -- the same correction slice 09 made to BreakevenStop.
if (ctx.BarsSinceEntry() == 0) minProf = ctx.OpenPositionProfit();
if (el_lt(ctx.OpenPositionProfit(), minProf)) minProf = ctx.OpenPositionProfit();
